  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 111 PM EDT Sat Aug 15 2026\n\nMuch like the last few days, scattered, short-lived showers and \nthunderstorms this afternoon and evening will have a low end \npotential for damaging wind gusts. Cells that track over the same \nlocations could result in some minor flooding issues. Patchy fog \nwill form tonight in areas that receive rain this afternoon, or in\nvalleys. \n\nThe mid/upper level ridge over the Deep South today retrogrades\nand flattens out tomorrow, keeping the track of MCSs in the OH\nValley/Great Lakes region to our north. The shift of the ridge \nappears to bring some drier air aloft, as PWs drop down to 1.3-1.6\ninches. Forecast soundings show a stronger cap atop a deeper \nmixed layer as well, so we should see less convective development \nin the afternoon compared to the last few days. Some isolated \nshowers/storms are possible in SW VA and the TN mountains. The\nreduced rain chance and cloud cover should result in ample diurnal\nheating that will bring temperatures into the 90s for most\nlocations. Although boundary layer mixing will lower afternoon\ndewpoints a bit (lower to mid 70s), we should still see Heat \nIndex values of 105-110 in the central and southern TN Valley. \n\nOn Sunday night, the NAM and some CAMS show remnant MCS \nconvection moving across southern WV, possibly clipping our SW VA\ncounties. The overnight timing will not be favorable for any \nsevere threat.\n\nA greater threat of severe storms may come on Monday. A shortwave\ntrough and a surface cold front will be crossing KY in the morning,\nlikely initiating convection as it moves closer to the Cumberland\nPlateau in the afternoon. The air mass ahead of the front will\nremain capped into the early afternoon and have plenty of MLCAPE,\naround 3000 J/kg, and there will be some shear present in \nnorthern sections, around 30-40 kt of 0-6 km shear shown by the \nNAM. Also concerning is the low level temp/dewpoint profile in \nmodel soundings (NAM and GFS), which show an inverted-V profile \nwith steep lapse rates and a deep boundary layer, favorable for \ndamaging wind gusts. DCAPE values in these soundings are 1000-1500\nJ/kg. The RRFS shows a pretty intense convective line moving \nthrough the area between 7 pm and 11 pm. The HRRR does not yet go \nout into Monday afternoon, but it does develop a convective \ncomplex in central and eastern KY in the morning. Wording in the\nHWO will be beefed up to address this potential severe threat. \n\nThe Heat Index on Monday may continue to be in the range for an\nAdvisory in the central and southern TN Valley, but will let the\nmid shift handle the extention if it is needed. The deep boundary\nlayer mixing that is expected on Monday may drop dewpoints further\nthan currently expected, and could keep the Heat Index just under\ncriteria. \n\nThe surface front pushes through the area Monday night. This\nshould bring some slightly lower temperatures and dewpoints for \nTuesday, ending the dangerous heat potential but still close to \nnormal temps. The ridge will continue to retrograde the rest of\nthe week, putting a broad trough across the eastern Conus. This\nwill increase our rain chances through the week as embedded\nshortwaves move through the larger trough and the jet stream\ndrops into the OH Valley.",
